ers most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example table: 26 January: arrival of the First Fleet 1788; seen as a celebration by some and as a day of mourning by ot Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample table: 26 January: arrival of the First Fleet 1788; seen as a celebration by some and as a day of mourning by others. 26 May: National Sorry Day; remembers Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children who were removed from families by government policies. July: NAIDOC Week; celebrates culture and achievements. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, m