with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAustralia Day on 26 January marks the arrival of the First Fleet at Sydney Cove in 1788. Many Australians celebrate it, and many First Nations people see it as the start of dispossession and call it Survival Day or Invasion Day. National Sorry Day on 26 May remembers the Stolen Generations. NAIDOC Week in July celebrates the history, culture and achievements of First Nations Peoples. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 28, Lesson 1: Australia Day, Sorry Day and NAIDOC Week","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Community and remembrance","week":28,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nE